  "read": {
    "headline": "The ten-year touched 5% for the first time since 2007. Six hours later the Fed decides.",
    "standfirst": "Tuesday put the whole board under the bond market: oil up five percent, the ten-year through 5.04% intraday, a record yield at the twenty-year auction, and every US sector red except energy. The hike at 14:00 ET is priced at 92–94%; the board below is what it has already done to positioning.",
    "points": [
      {
        "title": "The curve stopped being a front-end story on Tuesday.",
        "text": "For a month the repricing lived in the belly: the two- and three-year are up 11.5% and 12% over four weeks while the thirty-year is up under one. Tuesday was different. The ten-year rose 5bp to close at 5.00% after touching 5.04%, the twenty-year cleared at a record 5.42% at auction, and the thirty-year printed 5.36%. Every point on the curve rose together, which is why 2s10s held at 33bp instead of flattening further. The market is no longer only pricing one meeting — it is pricing what the ten-year is worth in a world where the Fed hikes into 3.4% inflation."
      },
      {
        "title": "Energy against consumer discretionary is the oil trade in equity clothing.",
        "text": "WTI rose 5.1% on Tuesday to $106.56 as fighting intensified in the Middle East, and has given back 1.4% this morning. Energy was the only US sector to close green, up 2.17%; consumer discretionary was the worst, down 1.75%. That pair is the cleanest expression of the inflation channel on the board: crude is up 23.5% in a month and 81% on the year, heating oil 136%. A member long discretionary and short energy is short oil whether they meant to be or not."
      },
      {
        "title": "The dollar has now confirmed what rates were saying.",
        "text": "Last edition the open question was why the dollar was not stronger with hike odds at 90%. It answered on Tuesday: the index reached 99.60, a two-week high, and EUR/USD fell for a fifth straight day to a one-month low near 1.153. Gold fell 0.4% to $4,335 in the New York session before bouncing this morning; it is still down 12.8% over six months. The divergence that looked like a tell has closed the other way — rates were right, and the metals and the euro caught up."
      },
      {
        "title": "Korea is the loudest number on the page, and it is a three-month number.",
        "text": "The Korea ETF is down 6.5% over five days and 13.9% over three months, and the KOSPI is down 24% over the same quarter in local terms. Taiwan is down 3.8% on the week. Both are still up 68–82% on the year, so the drawdown is inside an enormous move — but it is the AI hardware trade unwinding, and it is happening in the same week US technology fell 2.1% and momentum gave up 5% against the index in a month. If you own semis anywhere, you own this."
      },
      {
        "title": "Nothing in bonds has worked this year except floating paper.",
        "text": "The twenty-year Treasury ETF is down 7.4% on the year and 10.7% over twelve months; investment-grade corporate credit is down 7.1% over a year; even TIPS are down 3.75% year to date because real yields rose too. The single fixed-income line in the green is senior loans, up 0.19% on Tuesday and positive over one, three and six months. In a hiking cycle the coupon resets are the only thing on the bond side that gets paid for rates going up — everything with a fixed coupon is fighting the tide."
      },
      {
        "title": "Crypto is up 17–28% in a month while everything rate-sensitive fell.",
        "text": "Bitcoin is up 17% over a month, Ether 25%, Solana 27%, XRP 28% — and this is the month in which the ten-year added 6% and long Treasuries fell 0.8%. Whatever crypto is trading, it is not the discount rate. It has softened this week, down 2–7% over five days, so the flow is fading into the decision; but a month like that against a rates move like this is the strongest evidence on the board that these are not one position."
      }
    ]
  },
